CUDA 13.3 is the latest. cuda-toolkit-13 is the meta-package that tracks the latest (13.3). Here's the full procedure in order:

1. Stop the stack first
bash# Kill llama-server
kill $(pgrep llama-server)
# Stop OpenWebUI and Playwright from their terminal tabs, or just close those tabs

2. Install CUDA 13.3
bash
sudo apt install cuda-toolkit-13-3

This pulls in the compiler, libraries, and headers. CUDA 12.8 stays installed — they coexist.

3. Verify the symlink and nvcc version
bashls -la /usr/local/cuda
nvcc --version
The symlink should now point to cuda-13.3. If nvcc still reports 12.8, the symlink wasn't updated automatically — fix it with:
bash

sudo ln -sfn /usr/local/cuda-13.3 /usr/local/cuda
Then re-check nvcc --version.

4. Rebuild llama.cpp
bash

cd /home/sy5/llama.cpp/build
rm CMakeCache.txt
cmake .. -DGGML_CUDA=ON -DCMAKE_CUDA_ARCHITECTURES="89"
make -j$(nproc)

5. Verify the new binary
bash

/home/sy5/llama.cpp/build/bin/llama-server --version
Check the startup output confirms it was built with CUDA 13.

6. Restart via the launcher — use the launcher rather than a manual command to ensure all tabs come up correctly.
